    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §212, Judy L",
    "lawSection": "Judiciary Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7703\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the judiciary law, in relation to expanding the func-\ntions of the chief administrator of the courts to include the compila-\ntion of certain data with respect to ethnicity, race, disability, veter-\nan status, gender, gender identity, and sexual orientation by specific\njurisdiction and submit an annual report of his or her findings\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nTo provide lawmakers, judicial appointing authorities, and the public\nwith demographic information about the judicial branch of New York State\ngovernment.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would add a new paragraph (y) to subdivision 1 of\nsection 212 of the Judiciary Law, requiring the Chief Administrative\nJudge to collect, compile and publish an annual report on the demograph-\nic information about the judges and justices of New York's Unified Court\nSystem. The demographic information would include statistics about\nethnicity, race, disability, veteran status, gender, gender identity,\nand sexual orientation of currently sitting judges and justices.\n\nSection 2 of the bill is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAccording to the U.S. Census Bureau, New York is one of the most diverse\nstates in the country, and current population trends indicate that the\ndiversity of the state will continue to grow in the coming years. New\nYork's diversity is, in no small part, a reflection of our state's\nhistory as a welcome destination for people of all backgrounds.\n\nIt is imperative that New York's government be representative of the\nstate's diversity. This is particularly true of the judiciary, a co-e-\nqual branch of government that has profound impact on the lives of New\nYorkers. A diverse judicial branch helps ensure impartiality and builds\npublic confidence in the justice system.\n\nThe information that this legislation would require the Office of Court\nAdministration to annually report will help give the public, policymak-\ners, and judicial appointing authorities an accurate accounting of the\ndemographic makeup of New York's judicial branch.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone, new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
